  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1257 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nThis Afternoon and Evening\n\nA weakening mesoscale convective complex (MCC) will continue to\nweaken as it moves east toward southeast Minnesota and\nnortheast Iowa. As an outflow boundary (from last night's \nconvection) moves south and southwest ahead of this MCC, \nsurface-based CAPES will decrease from 2-3K J/kg to 1-2K J/kg. \nStill plenty of uncertainty on whether there will be any showers\nand storms. The CAMs indicate that there may be some scattered \nshowers and isolated storms west of US Highway 14. At this time,\nkept the forecast dry, but will keep an eye on the trends this \nafternoon.\n\nHigh temperatures this afternoon will range from the mid-70s to\nmid-80s.\n\nOvernight Tonight\n\nAs the nocturnal jet intersects a front across western Minnesota\nand central Iowa, showers and storms will develop across\nsouthwest Minnesota and north-central Iowa. As their cold pools\ngradually coalesce, a MCC will develop. This system will dive \nsouthward into a 1-2K J/kg surface-based CAPES located over \nwestern Iowa.\n\nWith light southeast winds in the lowest 2K feet late tonight,\nlow-level moisture will increase underneath a nocturnal \ninversion . This should result in the development of MVFR \nvisibilities due to fog and IFR/IFR ceilings. These should \ndevelop between 06.08z to 06.10z and dissipate between 06.14z \nand 06.16z.\n\nSunday \n\nThe MCC which develops to our west tonight will continue to move\nsouth into better instability across western Iowa on Sunday. \nSome of the CAMs suggest that maybe a few of these showers and \nstorms might move into areas west of US Highway 52. Due to this,\nkept a 20-30 percent chance of showers and storms in the \nforecast. Even if they do, both the 0-3 km and 0-6 km shear are \nless than 20 knots; thus, not expecting any severe weather.\n\nTuesday into Wednesday\n\nA vigorous shortwave trough will move east across the Northern\nPlains and Upper Mississippi River Valley. This will flatten the\nstrong ridge. Showers and storms will be found along a cold\nfront as it moves southeast through the region. With the better\n0-3 km and 0-6 km shear being located in the wake of the front,\nnot anticipating any severe weather.\n\nHigh temperatures will range from the upper 70s to mid-80s on\nTuesday and from the mid-70s to mid-80s on Wednesday. With zonal\nto northwest flow for the remainder of the week, temperatures\nwill be near-normal.",
